text:
O let me go among the people! I am weary;
Let me be free and wander where I will.
What is this world? It is a field of corn or wheat;
The grain is sown, it rises at my feet,
It bends toward me as I pass. So all men are born
To give their lives for others. O, how heavy

This world, my luvvy, what a cruel place!

It takes a man to be a woman's friend.

He has no sympathy with her sorrows;

His heart never shows any tender sign;

She needs his help, but it's too late when she calls—

Why didn't he come sooner ?But still he tells her that his heart Is all in pain;

And if she loved him, why would she not remain?

If this were life and love as they are made,

There wouldn't be so many women left forlorn

One mornin' early as I went out doors
I seed two men talkin' on my door step,
They said they'd like to know if I had any milk;
I answered in plain words what I did think.
" We wants no milk !" says one o' them two ;
The other then began to cry and sob;
Says he, " You can come into our kitchen
If you will please tell us where we are."
( This they all say is very bad luck).

The whole world was a nest
 With its warm eggs on my breast;
 There were no cares or worries at home--

The day was fair and mild when first we met;
But it is winter-time—the season is bleak;
It is not seldom known for rain or sleet;
Yet though our journey seems so long and steep,
In spite of storms, in spite of snows, and cold,
We meet once more.

In my youthfulness I was all-powerful;

I took no thought for what might follow;

And so I sailed into dangerous waters

Of life, which seemed most pleasant to swallow.

The soldiers beat him on every side;

And for his good health they took away all food.

As the story goes, in his later years Plato lived at Aegina and had no desire to return to Athens until after Aristotle’s death (322 B.C.). But even then he would not permit himself to be seen or heard except as an old man when a visitor wished to see him, so that people might think he had died earlier than he actually did (according to Diogenes Laërtius 10:46). In any event, from that time onward
